  "Mexican Cycad"
 ],
 "description": "Cardboard cycad is a slow-growing cycad with rigid, cardboard-textured leaves that is native to Mexico. It is a member of the zamia family (Zamiaceae). The species epithet is Latin for \"having brown scales like bran\" and refers to the scaly indumentum of the leaves and petioles. The showy, large leaves grow in a rosette pattern similar to palms. It is easy to grow in well-drained soils in full sun to partial shade. A large seed cone forms in the center of the female plant with many red, toxic seeds. They are drought- and salt-tolerant. In NC, it will need to be brought indoors for the winter. Grow this plant in well-drained sandy loam soil in full sun to partial shade. Allow the soil to dry between waterings and use a pot with several drainage holes. They are slow-growing, so they can make a nice indoor plant. More information on Zamia .",
